PART 3 — PROMPT ANATOMY AND STRUCTURAL SCHEMA
Omni 1.1 Flash operates most reliably when input prompts follow a predictable, structured anatomy1. Structuring the prompt prevents multi-token semantic collision—a phenomenon where visual modifiers blend unintentionally across subjects and backgrounds.
The standard structural formula maps as follows:
Subject + Subject Attributes + Action + Environment + Context/Background + Camera & Composition + Lens & Focus + Lighting & Atmosphere + Style & Visual Treatment + Motion Mechanics + Audio Descriptors + Timing & Sequence.
Consider an uncompiled text concept: "A huge black cat walks through an abandoned hospital at night." The compiled structural output expands this input into precise execution blocks:
A panther-sized domestic black cat with sleek short fur and glowing yellow eyes prowls slowly forward along a littered, vinyl-tiled corridor of an abandoned hospital. The camera tracks backward smoothly at eye level, maintaining a medium close-up shot with shallow depth of field. Moonlight streams through cracked high windows, casting long geometric shadows across peeling wall paint and floating dust particles in the air. Photorealistic 4K cinematic footage with realistic physical lighting. The cat's heavy paws make quiet, rhythmic thuds on the damp floor, accompanied by a low, distant wind howling through open doorways.
