PART 7 — MOTION ENGINE
Motion generation requires explicit physical direction. In Omni 1.1 Flash, vague action statements like "the car drives fast" produce temporal shearing or static warping1. Prompts must define primary subject motion, environmental motion, camera motion, and secondary physical reactions1.
Effective multi-layer motion requires structuring five distinct movement layers within the prompt:
    1. Primary Subject Vector: Direct physical mechanics (e.g., "panther prowls forward along the central axis").
    2. Physical Reaction Physics: Weight transfer and muscle dynamics (e.g., "weight shifts across shoulder blades, muscles flexing beneath fur").
    3. Environmental Motion: Atmospheric dynamics (e.g., "dust motes swirl in air, torn curtains sway softly").
    4. Secondary Entity Motion: Background movements (e.g., "water droplets drip periodically from ceiling beam").
    5. Camera Motion Trajectory: Optical tracking (e.g., "camera tracks backward smoothly at matching speed").
